From bb2983edacf813bf1b78aa109ba5bef67da47dfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunxiaopeng <sunxiaopeng@canaan-creative.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 12:14:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: canaan: add PDM microphone capture

Adds a capture-only sound card for PDM microphones wired to the K230 PDM
receiver. It is an independent path - no I2S controller, no internal
codec - so a board can run PDM capture alongside the existing card.

Up to eight microphones are supported. The receiver has four data
lines, and a single line carries two microphones when both clock edges
are sampled, which is the usual arrangement. The default configuration
is one data line on both edges, giving two channels.

The awkward part is the data format: every 32-bit FIFO word carries a
four-bit channel tag alongside its 24 bits of PCM, so the stream cannot
go straight into the buffer the application sees. PDMA fills a bounce
buffer instead and the period callback strips the tags into the ALSA
ring. Doing it this way rather than through an ASoC .copy callback keeps
mmap working, and with it dsnoop, at the cost of a second buffer of a
few tens of kilobytes.

The tags are used rather than ignored: the frame is reassembled by
looking at every word's tag instead of assuming the period starts on a
frame boundary, which it does not - the first burst after a FIFO clear
can begin on any channel. A stream that loses step then recovers at the
next channel 0 instead of swapping channels for the rest of the
recording.

Two supporting changes:

  - canaan_k230_audio.c gains the PDM register helpers and records the
    physical base. The PDM registers sit inside the block that driver
    already maps, so they have to be reached through it rather than
    ioremapped a second time. The probe path also stops storing a
    possible ERR_PTR in sai.base.

  - k230_peridma.c whitelists the PDM FIFO at 0x9140f4c0. The address
    check is [addr, addr+len), which a fixed FIFO address can never
    satisfy, so each one needs an explicit entry; the two existing magic
    numbers are named at the same time.

No board device tree is touched. The driver only probes where a board
adds a canaan,k230-audio-pdm node, so boards without PDM microphones
see no new sound card and no PDM clock driven onto their pins. The
binding documents how to add one.

canaan,pdm-io-group says which pins feed the receiver's first two
inputs. IO37 and IO35 carry the two I2S output data lines and PDM inputs
0 and 1 on the same alt function, and pdm_conf_0's input mode is the
second mux that separates them - a choice the pin controller cannot
reach, so it cannot come from pinctrl. Selecting I2S leaves inputs 0 and
1 on their other pins, IO27 and IO28; selecting PDM feeds them from IO37
and IO35 instead, and IO27 and IO28 then no longer reach the receiver
however pinctrl has muxed them. Inputs 2 and 3 exist only on IO36 and
IO34 and are outside this field.

A board on IO27 and IO28 therefore wants the I2S setting, which is what
the I2S card programs anyway, so PDM capture costs it nothing. Only a
board on IO35 and IO37 has to take the field, and it gives up I2S output
data on those two pins. Since the I2S card wants the same field and the
two probe in an order neither controls, canaan_k230_audio.c arbitrates:
the I2S side sets it only while the PDM card has not claimed it, and the
PDM card hands it back on remove. The internal codec bypass bit is a
separate matter and is left to the I2S card - the codec is fed inside
the block, not through these pins, and PDM capture does not disturb it.
